WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.150
You can probably recognize this letter, even with pieces missing.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.070
Can a network do the same?

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.618
This is a Hopfield network.

00:00:10.618 --> 00:00:15.514
We'll give it three patterns to remember: H, T, and X.

00:00:16.614 --> 00:00:20.380
Each pixel becomes a neuron, either on or off.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.982
Every neuron connects to every other neuron.

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:28.450
Learning changes the strength of those connections.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:33.602
Together, the connections encode the patterns we want the network to remember.

00:00:34.702 --> 00:00:37.034
Now we give it a damaged letter.

00:00:37.034 --> 00:00:42.790
One neuron at a time, the network checks its connections and updates its state.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.140
We can track this process with a number called energy.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.524
As the network updates, energy never rises.

00:00:51.524 --> 00:00:54.594
Eventually, the pattern stops changing.

00:00:55.694 --> 00:00:59.839
But settling down doesn't always mean remembering correctly.

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:03.086
Too much noise can lead to a different pattern.

00:01:04.186 --> 00:01:05.777
Now try it yourself.

00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:11.026
Damage a letter, press Recall, and see what the network remembers.